00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:22.363
is, and try to bring awareness.

00:07:22.363 --> 00:07:24.280
JESSICA DENE EARLEY-CHA:
So thank you so much.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:26.238
I'm a developer advocate
for actions on Google,

00:07:26.238 --> 00:07:28.680
which means I get to build
a lot of voice applications.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.702
And through that, I build a lot
of content from the learnings

00:07:31.702 --> 00:07:33.660
that I get, so we could
teach other third party

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:35.910
developers how to build
for Google Assistant.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:38.040
And then with that, I
also get all the feedback

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:39.750
from developers
on what goes great

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:43.050
and what goes not so great and
bring that back to the product

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:44.400
team so we can make it better.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.442
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Gotcha.

00:07:45.442 --> 00:07:47.220
So first things
first, Cathy, what

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.710
is conversation design, right?

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:52.050
You mentioned that you
kind of focus on that.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:53.152
I don't know what that is.

00:07:53.152 --> 00:07:54.300
CATHY PEARL: That is
an excellent question

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:55.680
that I'm asked many times.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.840
Basically, you can
think of it as we

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.710
are trying to teach computers to
communicate like humans and not

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:02.800
the other way around.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:04.920
So basically,
rather than have you

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:08.700
learn some complex way of either
speaking or typing or tapping

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.030
or swiping, we'd rather leverage
how humans have been speaking

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:15.240
for, what, 150,000 years
and make it that much easier

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.007
to get things done, get
the information you need,

00:08:18.007 --> 00:08:19.840
just the way you're
naturally used to doing.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:21.215
And so we want to
work in a world

00:08:21.215 --> 00:08:23.820
where we're thinking about
how humans communicate

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:26.790
and then applying that within
the technical constraints that

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:28.120
exist today.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:30.490
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So I
have a question about that.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:34.049
So I think we got
the definition of how

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:36.539
to work with
conversation design,

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:39.150
but Jessica, I have
this question for you.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:44.580
Is this any different than how
we interact with maybe, say,

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:45.840
apps or websites?

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:48.630
And do developers have
to do something specific

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:50.800
when they develop for voice?

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:51.270
JESSICA DENE EARLEY-CHA:
Oh, definitely.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.478
Developing for voice is very
different than something

00:08:53.478 --> 00:08:54.110
with a GUI.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.277
Because there's a lot of
control as a developer when

00:08:56.277 --> 00:08:56.910
you have a GUI.

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:59.625
There's only so many
buttons a user could push.

00:08:59.625 --> 00:09:01.500
Versus a conversation--
at any point, someone

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.150
could say anything
and you're supposed

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:05.190
to support the
user through that.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:07.800
And it's not just necessarily
that they could say anything

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:08.640
at any time.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.880
Because most people-- what
I've learned from Cathy--

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:11.550
they're cooperative.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:13.830
They want to support you and
be able to talk through things.

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:15.788
They're not going to be
just bringing something

00:09:15.788 --> 00:09:17.100
crazy into the conversation.

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.290
It's more on how
people explain things

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.720
or how they communicate
could be slightly different.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.330
And our voice applications,
or actions that we call them

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:27.580
in Google Assistant-- we call
those actions instead of apps--

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:30.390
we have to figure out how to
capture those different types

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:33.018
of utterances that users
can say and give them

00:09:33.018 --> 00:09:34.060
the appropriate response.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:36.553
So it's definitely a
different mental shift

00:09:36.553 --> 00:09:38.470
when it comes to thinking
about how you build.

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:40.920
And something that I've
seen and I actually

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.350
experienced when I made my first
application was I built it,

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:46.400
and as I was going to
the docs, it said Design.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.465
And I was like, that's
cool, I'll do that later.

00:09:48.465 --> 00:09:50.590
Because as a web developer,
that's what I would do.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.370
I would kind of build
all the scaffolding,

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.690
then I bring in all
the design after.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:56.910
And it turns out, nope,
can't do that this time.

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:59.160
You have to have the
conversation design first,

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:00.788
and then you build after.

00:10:00.788 --> 00:10:03.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So are there
like a list of best practices?

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:05.790
Are there a bunch of
design docs on how

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:07.152
to do conversation design?

00:10:07.152 --> 00:10:08.610
CATHY PEARL: Yeah,
we actually have

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.870
established a lot of best
practices and principles.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:13.808
You can go to, for example,
actions.google.com/design.

00:10:13.808 --> 00:10:16.350
Get a whole ton of information
of even what is it, how to get

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:17.233
started.

00:10:17.233 --> 00:10:19.150
The first thing we always
recommend to people,

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:21.450
whether you're a
developer, or a marketer,

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:23.340
or whoever, whatever
role you have,

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:25.775
is to start with something
called sample dialogues.

00:10:25.775 --> 00:10:27.150
And what that
means is basically,

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:28.778
you're creating a conversation.

00:10:28.778 --> 00:10:29.820
It's like a movie script.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:33.990
It's a back and forth between
the user and the action.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:35.723
And that just really
helps you sort out

00:10:35.723 --> 00:10:37.890
the complexities of what
this is going to look like.

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:39.270
Because a lot of times,
like Jessica mentioned,

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:40.937
people think, oh,
yeah, the words, we'll

00:10:40.937 --> 00:10:42.070
just fix those later.

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:44.430
But in fact, the words
are the structure.

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:46.770
And it's going to impact
the flow and the wording

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:49.330
and everything throughout
your whole development cycle.

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:51.030
So you might as well start
with design in the beginning

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:53.110
and really save yourself
a lot of headaches.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:56.880
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So can anyone
write these conversation design

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:57.870
practices?

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:01.530
Or when we start to develop,
is it just the developer?

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:03.090
Who are the
stakeholders involved

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:05.910
when somebody is thinking
about designing something,

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:07.363
like a conversation?

00:11:07.363 --> 00:11:09.030
CATHY PEARL: I think
in the ideal world,

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:12.510
a team will have a conversation
designer as part of it.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:14.700
And that person can really
help steer the direction

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.670
of how things should sound, what
things we need to keep in mind,

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:18.960
what are we going to need
from the back end, all

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:20.110
that kind of stuff.

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:21.610
Now I understand
in the real world,

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.485
not everybody is going
to have a conversation

00:11:23.485 --> 00:11:25.410
designer to work with.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:27.420
So instead, in those
cases, I definitely

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:30.750
encourage the developer or the
project manager, whoever it is,

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:32.940
to really study up
on best practices

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:35.312
and then write sample dialogues.

00:11:35.312 --> 00:11:37.770
And then what you do is just
like in Hollywood with a movie

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:38.820
script, you do a table read.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:39.780
You read them out loud.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:42.520
Because sometimes
we write formally.

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:44.520
And when we actually hear
those things out loud,

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:45.890
they sound kind of
stiff and awkward.

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:48.000
So you really want to read
out loud with other people,

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.542
and you'll quickly get a feel
for where things are going well

00:11:50.542 --> 00:11:52.685
and where things
are going wrong.

00:11:52.685 --> 00:11:54.810
And that can really help
anybody really get started

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:55.845
and do a better job.

00:11:55.845 --> 00:11:57.720
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah,
I think the last time

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.060
we chatted with Priyanka
a little bit about some

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:03.310
of the other chat options
specifically built into Google,

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:06.150
we kind of brought up the idea
of people being frustrated

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.740
by being very locked into
kind of an older school

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:11.530
version of a voice system, where
it wants you to say something

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:12.400
very exactly.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:16.570
Like, I want one small cup
of coffee that is dark brew,

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:17.620
please.

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:19.780
And it adds a lot more
effort into it, as opposed

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:21.910
to how people might
more naturally speak.

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:24.250
You mentioned earlier,
Jessica, that you

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:26.170
thought about the
code part of it first

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:27.640
and the design part of it later.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:31.270
And that can lead to a lot of
challenges using these best

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:31.910
practices.

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:33.880
How do you kind of
convince somebody

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:35.930
to actually take
them into account

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:37.870
and actually spend the
time upfront working

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:41.050
with them, before just kind of
trotting off and doing the code

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:41.650
part?

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:42.250
JESSICA DENE EARLEY-CHA:
Oh, yeah, that's

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:44.660
something that I constantly
see whenever I go out,

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:46.760
especially when I do
a one-day workshop.

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:49.930
It's really unrealistic to have
a great design and great voice

00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:51.680
application built in one day.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:52.970
It takes a long time.

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:56.440
And so I definitely try to do my
little soapbox speech of like,

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:58.240
oh my gosh, I spent
hours thinking

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:00.400
these are technical
issues when I was building

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:01.570
prior to having a design.

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:02.950
It turns out was a design issue.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:05.470
I didn't know where I was going
in regards to the conversation

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:05.970
flow.

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:08.360
So I think that's
kind of my mantra.

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:10.360
That's what I'm constantly
trying to figure out.

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:12.820
I don't think I have a great
kind of convincing argument

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:15.833
besides sharing my story and
sharing kind of the statistics

00:13:15.833 --> 00:13:17.500
on, like, it takes
more time, and you're

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:20.140
going to end up spending more
developer time in the long run

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:21.890
trying to fix those mistakes.

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:23.670
And because voice
is still fairly new,

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:26.620
I think it's more people are
going to be experiencing that.

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:29.830
And then they realize and they
go, oh, Jessica and Cathy, they

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:30.333
were right.

00:13:30.333 --> 00:13:32.000
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
is there an example

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:33.130
you can share of
where someone might

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:34.963
have run into that or
an example that you've

00:13:34.963 --> 00:13:35.880
run into specifically?

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:38.088
JESSICA DENE EARLEY-CHA: Oh
yeah, my very first voice

00:13:38.088 --> 00:13:40.180
application that I built.
The point of application

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:43.065
was to mock or mimic
technical interviews.

00:13:43.065 --> 00:13:45.190
Because the hard part when
it comes to interviewing

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:47.830
is not so much being able to
solve the technical problems,

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:49.960
it's hearing the problems and
practicing it, and hearing it

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:50.600
from someone else.

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:50.830
I was like, cool.

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:51.740
I'll build this thing.

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:52.630
And it's a really cool tool.

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:54.088
I would have loved
to have had this

00:13:54.088 --> 00:13:56.560
when I was going through my
process of looking for a job.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:59.290
And I would get into these
weird kind of scuffles

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:01.030
where I would start,
but how would I

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:03.880
bring the user from giving
them the information,

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:06.340
bringing them back, and letting
them know how to come back.

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:07.923
And I knew how to
do that technically,

00:14:07.923 --> 00:14:10.540
but when I sent it out
and had people try it out,

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:12.080
it wasn't natural.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:13.080
It felt weird.

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:16.000
I was like, well, how can I be
clever and put in little flags

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:17.177
and try to bring them back?

00:14:17.177 --> 00:14:18.260
And it's like, oh my gosh.

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:21.490
Why am I doing all this overhead
to solve a problem where

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:23.243
I could have just
stopped and went OK,

00:14:23.243 --> 00:14:24.410
let me talk to other people.

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:26.980
Let me figure out and build
the actual conversation design

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:28.390
by using sample dialogues.

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:30.640
And I think something that
could be really challenging

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:31.870
or something that--

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:34.360
I know when I mention to
folks, especially developers,

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:38.240
build some sample dialogues,
I get the, well, what is that?

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:40.840
It's like, it's
literally writing down--

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:43.000
pretend you're the action
and pretend someone else

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:45.260
is the user and having
that conversation.

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:48.460
So I usually like telling
people, just grab two friends.

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:50.500
Tell one of the friends
you are the action.

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:52.170
This is what you do.

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:53.910
The other friend,
you are the user.

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:54.682
Now talk.

00:14:54.682 --> 00:14:56.890
And just let them naturally
talk and write that down,

00:14:56.890 --> 00:15:01.025
and how they're doing it is
the best free way to do that.

00:15:01.025 --> 00:15:02.650
CATHY PEARL: Yeah,
and just to give you

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:05.230
a really basic example, I think
a lot about yes/no questions

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:07.173
and how often we fail
the user on those.

00:15:07.173 --> 00:15:08.840
Because a lot of
times, people will say,

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:10.382
well, I asked them
a yes/no question.

00:15:10.382 --> 00:15:12.317
So let's say I asked
you, do you like candy?

00:15:12.317 --> 00:15:14.150
And you might say yes,
and you might say no.

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:15.075
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Yes, I do like candy.

00:15:15.075 --> 00:15:17.033
CATHY PEARL: So you said,
yes, I do like candy.

00:15:17.033 --> 00:15:18.067
You didn't say, yes.

00:15:18.067 --> 00:15:19.900
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I
just broke the chat bot.

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:20.560
CATHY PEARL: Well,
that's the point.

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.977
You shouldn't have broken it
if somebody designed it well.

00:15:22.977 --> 00:15:25.850
Or I might even say, like,
well, do you count chocolate?

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:28.450
Which is neither a yes or a no,
but it's a very natural thing

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:29.748
for someone to say.

00:15:29.748 --> 00:15:31.540
And so someone who's
really thinking things

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:33.442
through and following
best practices

00:15:33.442 --> 00:15:34.900
is not just going
to be like, oh, I

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:36.370
told them to say yes or no.

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:37.800
That's all I'm going to accept.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:38.770
They're going to
say, hm, what are

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:39.940
related things they might say?

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:41.730
Or if maybe you're booking
a table at a restaurant,

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:43.630
and you say, how many
people in your party?

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:46.000
And maybe I say, well, do
you have outdoor seating?

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:48.580
Now that's a related question,
but it's not a number,

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:49.540
like you asked me for.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:51.498
That being said, I'm not
going to suddenly say,

00:15:51.498 --> 00:15:53.290
how tall is Barack
Obama when you asked me,

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:54.706
how many people
are in your party?

00:15:54.706 --> 00:15:56.740
So that's what we call
sort of adjacent topics.

00:15:56.740 --> 00:15:58.763
So you might not enter
the exact question,

00:15:58.763 --> 00:16:00.430
but the user will say
something related,

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:01.640
and you need to
anticipate those,

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:03.510
or things are going
to fail very quickly.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:04.840
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah, I think
this is something, Priyanka,

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:06.757
you brought up the last
time we chatted about.

00:16:06.757 --> 00:16:09.370
People don't know exactly
how to have a conversation.

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:10.370
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:11.270
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I think,
which is, first of all,

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:12.100
a fascinating subject.

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:13.400
I mean, Jessica, you
were mentioning earlier,

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:15.760
if you tell two people to
have a conversation, boy, they

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:17.280
get really awkward really
quickly, don't they?

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:17.346
JESSICA DENE
EARLEY-CHA: Oh, yeah.

00:16:17.346 --> 00:16:18.950
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Because they
think about everything you say.

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:21.550
But people don't normally think
about everything they say.

00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:23.680
They just kind of keep talking,
and eventually, their brain

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:24.400
might catch up.

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:26.050
But for people
like me, no, words

00:16:26.050 --> 00:16:27.580
are way faster than thought.

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:28.930
So you can just keep on talking.

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:30.180
But it's an interesting thing.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:31.850
Because when you
actually call it out,

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:34.970
I don't think a lot of people
do think about how much we use,

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:36.340
especially contextually, right?

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:38.132
Which is one of the
hardest things to grab,

00:16:38.132 --> 00:16:41.000
but one of the most important
pieces of a conversation.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.270
Because you can ask a
question like exactly that--

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:44.240
do you have outdoor seating?

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:45.760
It's like, oh, well, you're
talking about this restaurant.

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:46.930
You're talking about this.

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:48.550
How do you handle
that kind of idea

00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:51.445
that people don't know how
to have a conversation?

00:16:51.445 --> 00:16:53.320
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah,
I think it goes back

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:56.950
to the point where you don't
just start coding, right?

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:58.810
You start to think
about how it's

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:02.650
going to have interaction
with not just one person,

00:17:02.650 --> 00:17:04.510
but different people, right?

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:07.200
Because I could talk about
things in a different way.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.130
And I think we talked
about this last time

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.220
when we were like,
if I want coffee,

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:15.240
depending on the time of the day
and the day and the frustration

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:18.280
amount, I would ask for
coffee in different ways.

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:21.900
When I'm happy, I'd be like,
wow, coffee, this is amazing.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:24.510
Oh my gosh, can I just
get coffee right now?

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:25.720
I'm so frustrated.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:28.440
Like, things like
that, I think we

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.500
have to incorporate
how a user could

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:33.280
react in different
situations and ask

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:36.070
for the same thing
in different ways.

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:39.900
And then I guess, people
coming from different places

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:43.500
with different accents
could ask for certain things

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:45.880
in different ways,
so things like that.

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:47.890
But Cathy, I would
like to ask you,

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:50.940
what are the things that are
hard to deal with when we

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:52.325
are working with conversation?

00:17:52.325 --> 00:17:54.700
CATHY PEARL: I think one of
the things that's difficult--

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:56.220
and Jessica was talking
about the difference between

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:58.590
something like a GUI versus
a VUI, as we call it,

00:17:58.590 --> 00:17:59.970
Voice User Interface--

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:02.563
is the fact that with a
GUI, you know what they did.

00:18:02.563 --> 00:18:03.480
They pressed a button.

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:03.980
They swiped.

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:05.520
They chose a menu item.

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:08.070
With voice and natural
language understanding,

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:10.110
it's a little bit more
of an educated guess.

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:12.000
You know, speech
recognition accuracy

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:13.980
has improved
dramatically in the time

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:16.290
I've been working in this
field, but it's not perfect.

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:19.530
And human speech, we don't get
it right all the time either.

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:22.710
But when humans have
a misunderstanding,

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:26.280
we are really good at what we
call conversational repair.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:28.050
If you say something
like, oh, I went

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:30.120
to the such and such restaurant
last night, I didn't hear that.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:31.170
I'd say, what restaurant?

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:33.087
And you'd say, oh, this
one, and we'd move on.

00:18:33.087 --> 00:18:34.879
And we're very good at
that kind of repair.

00:18:34.879 --> 00:18:37.462
And that's something you really
have to anticipate when you're

00:18:37.462 --> 00:18:39.310
developing these
conversations, which is,

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:41.143
no matter how good your
design is, no matter

00:18:41.143 --> 00:18:42.840
how good your
prompts are, people

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:45.180
are going to say things
that you did not expect.

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:46.680
And you will get an error.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:49.080
And you need to spend actual
time thinking through,

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:52.140
what is my strategy for handling
when things go off the rails?

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:53.640
How do I get somebody
back on track?

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:55.390
And just doing the
default of like, sorry,

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:58.320
I didn't understand, please
say that again, is terrible.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:18:59.390
It won't work.

00:18:59.390 --> 00:19:01.030
And but we see it all the time.

00:19:01.030 --> 00:19:01.410
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
It's a great way

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:03.540
to get your blood
pressure up real quick.

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:04.450
CATHY PEARL: Exactly.

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:05.120
And so we were able to think.

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:06.300
We were like, well,
what's the user doing?

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:07.530
What did we just ask them?

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:09.150
Let's rephrase the
question a bit.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:10.010
Oh, we have another error.

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:11.530
OK, let's give them
more information.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:12.900
Maybe they can't find
their account number,

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:14.310
and we need to tell them more.

00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:17.010
You have to know upfront
you're going to spend time

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:18.900
on error handling or repair.

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:21.000
And that is going to
be the make it or break

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:23.370
it difference between your
users having a successful

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:24.570
experience or not.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:25.050
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Yeah, it was kind of

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:26.675
mentioned earlier
that sometimes people

00:19:26.675 --> 00:19:29.010
will say something
completely unrelated to what

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:30.120
you were asking about.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:31.660
Is that handled in the same way?

00:19:31.660 --> 00:19:35.612
Do you try to handle it in a,
like, context shifting way,

00:19:35.612 --> 00:19:37.320
and you say now we're
talking about this,

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:38.910
so the AI just has
to keep up, or do you

00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:40.327
try to keep them
in line with what

00:19:40.327 --> 00:19:42.180
the original conversation was?

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:43.320
CATHY PEARL: It kind
of depends on the goal.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:45.000
It's like, if you're
trying to book a table,

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:46.650
then you do want to get them
through those questions.

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:47.775
But you should be flexible.

00:19:47.775 --> 00:19:50.250
You have to answer the questions
in the exact same order.

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:52.853
On the other hand, if they're
saying something completely out

00:19:52.853 --> 00:19:54.520
of context, you may
not be able to help.

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:55.978
But another thing
I want to mention

00:19:55.978 --> 00:19:58.950
is basically, if somebody says
something that you can't do,

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:00.440
acknowledgment is
really important.

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:03.310
So for example, if I go to a
concierge at a hotel and I say,

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:05.318
hey, can you rent a car for me?

00:20:05.318 --> 00:20:06.360
And let's say they can't.

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:07.830
Let's say they only do
dinner reservations.

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:09.663
If the person just said,
I don't understand.

00:20:09.663 --> 00:20:11.150
Do you want dinner reservations?

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:12.540
I'd be like, no, I
want to rent a car.

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:14.498
What the human will
actually say is, I'm sorry.

00:20:14.498 --> 00:20:15.290
I can't rent a car.

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:16.748
Would you like
dinner reservations?

00:20:16.748 --> 00:20:19.350
So our systems, if you look in
the logs, and a lot of people

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:21.552
are requesting a feature
that you do not have,

00:20:21.552 --> 00:20:23.010
don't just say, I
don't understand.

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:24.302
You should do this other thing.

00:20:24.302 --> 00:20:26.850
Say, oh, sorry, we don't
do that feature yet.

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:28.170
I can do this or that.

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:29.822
People like to be acknowledged.

00:20:29.822 --> 00:20:32.280
When you have a frustrating
day and you're talking about it

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:34.450
with somebody, you don't
want them to say, well,

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:35.430
I don't know how to help you.

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:36.780
You want them to
say, wow, I hear

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:38.572
you're saying that you
had a difficult talk

00:20:38.572 --> 00:20:39.720
with your coworker.

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:42.420
People want to be acknowledged,
and this is true even

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:43.810
for a voice user interface.

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:44.790
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
it's incredibly important

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:47.220
to make sure that the
conversation system is

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:49.420
very clear about what
it can and can't handle,

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:52.890
but also that it still tries
to keep users down that path

00:20:52.890 --> 00:20:55.167
while matching all of the
kind of requests of that.

00:20:55.167 --> 00:20:57.000
I mean, I'm assuming
this is kind of covered

00:20:57.000 --> 00:20:58.740
in those best
practices of things

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:01.083
to keep in mind when
you're designing a system.

00:21:01.083 --> 00:21:02.250
CATHY PEARL: Yeah, for sure.

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:04.583
Like you said, you want to
further them towards the goal

00:21:04.583 --> 00:21:06.840
while, at the same
time, keeping them

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:09.270
from having a frustrating
experience and sort of gently

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:10.395
guiding them back on track.

00:21:10.395 --> 00:21:12.770
JESSICA DENE EARLEY-CHA: Yeah,
and most third party voice

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:14.400
applications don't
do everything.

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:16.783
They don't necessarily and
shouldn't replicate the Google

00:21:16.783 --> 00:21:18.450
Assistant because
Google Assistant could

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:19.710
do all the things.

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:21.240
Generally, a lot
of web developers

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:24.530
are building are specific to
a niche or to a certain topic.

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:25.730
And so that's where I know--

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:27.480
I've built server
applications-- and there

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:30.608
are times where users
are not as knowledgeable,

00:21:30.608 --> 00:21:32.400
that I can't tell a
joke because that's not

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:33.510
what the action's about.

00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:35.973
But most likely, the user
is thinking that they're

00:21:35.973 --> 00:21:37.140
talking to Google Assistant.

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:39.940
And so I have an intent in
several of my actions going,

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:41.820
hey, you might be
wanting Google Assistant.

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:43.560
Just say goodbye, and
it'll get you out.

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:45.750
And when you want to
chat about x, say, hey,

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:46.860
Google, talk to blah.

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:49.620
And that way, it can get the
user to what they want to do.

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:51.567
And it's not forcing me
as a developer trying

00:21:51.567 --> 00:21:53.400
to solve all the problems
because that's not

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:55.280
the purpose of
individual actions.

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:56.280
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:21:56.280 --> 00:22:00.270
And so what are some of the
common pitfalls that developers

00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:03.290
actually come across when
they're trying to develop?

00:22:03.290 --> 00:22:06.730
Because I think we talked a
little bit with Cathy about how

00:22:06.730 --> 00:22:09.190
do you actually
repair a conversation

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:13.390
with the conversation
design aspect of it?

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:17.320
But we didn't touch a little
bit on the developer aspects,

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:19.060
and what do they
see as challenges?

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:20.560
JESSICA DENE
EARLEY-CHA: I would say

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:23.980
the biggest challenge is not
choosing the right use case.

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:25.510
Doing things through
voice is great,

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:28.385
but it's not helpful when
you're searching for something

00:22:28.385 --> 00:22:30.010
particular, and
there's lots of options

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:31.510
and you're trying
to narrow it down.

00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:33.610
It might be helpful to
narrow it down using voice,

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:35.068
but then maybe at
the end, the user

00:22:35.068 --> 00:22:37.930
might want to be able to see the
last three options because they

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:39.220
want to get into the details.

00:22:39.220 --> 00:22:41.170
And that's where you
could use multi-modal.

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:44.530
Having a smart display or being
able to show it on a smartphone

00:22:44.530 --> 00:22:47.712
might be a helpful setup,
instead of just depending all

00:22:47.712 --> 00:22:50.170
on voice because that might
not be necessarily the best use

00:22:50.170 --> 00:22:51.243
case for that.

00:22:51.243 --> 00:22:52.660
And so I think the
best use case--

00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:53.650
and I usually tell
folks, if it's

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:55.900
something you're already
doing with your voice, that's

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:57.610
a great thing to
make into an action.

00:22:57.610 --> 00:22:59.710
If it's something like
you're using, let's say,

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:02.950
spreadsheets, that might be a
little more challenging to go,

00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:07.072
OK, if I want to get
column 4 and Row B

00:23:07.072 --> 00:23:09.530
and trying to figure that out,
if your spreadsheet is smart

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:12.497
and you have actual labels on
things and it could make sense,

00:23:12.497 --> 00:23:14.080
then that would make
a lot more sense.

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:16.288
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: You
brought up a great point there

00:23:16.288 --> 00:23:19.570
about multi-modality, and with
the new devices out there,

00:23:19.570 --> 00:23:21.610
we are getting into that world.

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:24.010
So Cathy, would you mind
touching a little bit

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:28.618
on how do you design properly
for those multi-modal devices?

00:23:28.618 --> 00:23:30.160
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
And just for anyone

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:33.460
who may not be sure
what multi-modal means,

00:23:33.460 --> 00:23:35.500
a definition would be fantastic.

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:37.683
Not for me, but
for everyone else.

00:23:37.683 --> 00:23:39.100
CATHY PEARL: So
multi-modal, we're

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:40.900
generally referring
to the case where

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:44.000
there are different ways you can
interact with the technology.

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:46.708
So for example, Jessica
mentioned smart displays,

00:23:46.708 --> 00:23:47.750
like the Google Home Hub.

00:23:47.750 --> 00:23:50.443
So that is what we call
a voice forward device.

00:23:50.443 --> 00:23:52.360
So you can do a lot of
things with your voice,

00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:54.450
but there is the
addition of a screen.

00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:55.540
And so you can see things.

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:57.500
Like, if you're shopping
for a blue shirt,

00:23:57.500 --> 00:23:58.510
you can see the
pictures on there.

00:23:58.510 --> 00:23:59.770
And something like
a phone, of course,

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:01.930
is also multi-modal because
you can type, you can speak,

00:24:01.930 --> 00:24:03.080
you can tap, you can swipe.

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:04.663
So when we're thinking
about designing

00:24:04.663 --> 00:24:06.640
for these new surfaces,
it's kind of exciting

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:08.848
because something like the
smart display is very new.

00:24:08.848 --> 00:24:11.620
And we haven't quite all figured
out exactly what all the best

00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:12.700
use cases are yet.

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:15.010
But generally,
our guidelines are

00:24:15.010 --> 00:24:16.593
that when you are
thinking about this,

00:24:16.593 --> 00:24:18.177
the first thing you
need to understand

00:24:18.177 --> 00:24:20.380
is that although it would
be great, unfortunately,

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:23.290
you can't just design once
and be done for all surfaces.

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:24.190
Obviously, there
are things that are

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:25.660
in common across all
of them, but there will

00:24:25.660 --> 00:24:27.130
be some differences as well.

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:30.552
So our advice is to start with
the voice first experience,

00:24:30.552 --> 00:24:31.510
like the smart speaker.

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:34.540
Because believe it or not,
that is often the most complex

00:24:34.540 --> 00:24:36.580
or maybe most constrained case.

00:24:36.580 --> 00:24:38.830
And if you get the
conversation done right

00:24:38.830 --> 00:24:40.525
for the smart speaker,
then what you do

00:24:40.525 --> 00:24:42.400
is you go to something
like the smart display

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:45.490
and you say, OK, now we have
the option for visual elements.

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:47.380
Where would it benefit
the conversation

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:49.575
to add visual components
at this point?

00:24:49.575 --> 00:24:51.700
And then you can do the
same for the phone as well.

00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:54.580
So start with the
voice only experience

00:24:54.580 --> 00:24:56.140
and expand from there.

00:24:56.140 --> 00:24:57.730
A lot of times,
people go with, oh,

00:24:57.730 --> 00:24:59.140
we're going to have the
pretty pictures first,

00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:00.670
and then they get back to
smart speaker and they're like,

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:01.880
I don't know what to do.

00:25:01.880 --> 00:25:03.328
So definitely start with voice.

00:25:03.328 --> 00:25:05.620
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well, it
also adds a tremendous level

00:25:05.620 --> 00:25:06.970
of complexity when
you're talking

00:25:06.970 --> 00:25:09.345
about mixing and matching all
these different interfaces.

00:25:09.345 --> 00:25:12.700
Not only is it now you're
doing conversation design,

00:25:12.700 --> 00:25:15.370
you're also doing all the
actual interface design.

00:25:15.370 --> 00:25:17.930
And then I have to imagine
it's exponential in terms

00:25:17.930 --> 00:25:19.930
of difficulty because
you're trying to mix them.

00:25:19.930 --> 00:25:22.750
And there's enough '80s
sci-fi shows out there

00:25:22.750 --> 00:25:25.030
that kind of showed people
interacting with computers

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:26.447
and then talking
to the computers,

00:25:26.447 --> 00:25:29.560
and maybe even using some
kind of third party tool, all

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:31.930
of these things mixing
together to kind of create

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:33.640
one single experience.

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:35.780
That's got to be hell
for UI designers,

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:38.710
whether it's graphical,
visual, or a combination.

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:40.480
What does that look
like right now?

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:42.813
CATHY PEARL: I don't know if
I'd use the words that it's

00:25:42.813 --> 00:25:44.080
hell, but, um.

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:46.040
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It's
a significant challenge.

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:46.630
CATHY PEARL: No, you're right.

00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:48.588
And I think it's important
to acknowledge that.

00:25:48.588 --> 00:25:50.590
And if you are, for
example, running a business

00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:53.380
or you're the person who's
the decision maker about we're

00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:55.235
going to do this
conversation action,

00:25:55.235 --> 00:25:56.860
you just have to keep
in mind that it's

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:57.670
going to take some time.

00:25:57.670 --> 00:25:59.710
And you have to have these
different skill sets.

00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:01.590
So you have to have
a visual designer.

00:26:01.590 --> 00:26:03.340
You have to have these
different folks who

00:26:03.340 --> 00:26:04.300
are going to work together.

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:06.610
And we have established
some of these nice processes

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:07.870
where there's handoffs.

00:26:07.870 --> 00:26:09.970
And so we really are
establishing these best

00:26:09.970 --> 00:26:12.190
practices to deal with these
new multi-modal services

00:26:12.190 --> 00:26:14.050
because as you say,
it does add complexity

00:26:14.050 --> 00:26:15.770
when you've got to be thinking
about all these things.

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:18.580
But again, if you go through
the process in the right order,

00:26:18.580 --> 00:26:20.758
you'll save yourself a lot
of headaches and hassles.

00:26:20.758 --> 00:26:22.050
And it'll be a little smoother.

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:22.750
JESSICA DENE EARLEY-CHA:
Mm-hmm, definitely.

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:25.720
And I would say even just
building a voice conversation

00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:29.920
application, it's not where
you could just work in a silo

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:30.680
all by yourself.

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:32.620
It's really working with
the different parties

00:26:32.620 --> 00:26:33.560
and working together.

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:35.950
So I definitely think
leading with the voice

00:26:35.950 --> 00:26:38.470
first, building that
experience first lays down

00:26:38.470 --> 00:26:41.210
a lot of great groundwork,
and then adding the visuals,

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:42.910
the complement, the support.

00:26:42.910 --> 00:26:44.710
And even the visuals,
we do have a new tool

00:26:44.710 --> 00:26:48.100
called Canvas, where
you can bring in HTML,

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:51.460
CSS, JavaScript into play,
which is a totally completely

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:52.233
new experience.

00:26:52.233 --> 00:26:53.650
And you can play
games and do lots

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:55.180
of fun interactive
simulations, too,

00:26:55.180 --> 00:26:57.143
to really make the
experience more rich.

00:26:57.143 --> 00:26:58.810
But definitely want
to start with voice,

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:01.080
and then you can add in
those fun features later.

00:27:01.080 --> 00:27:03.080
But I wanted to bring up
the Canvas because even

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:04.810
with Canvas, you
don't necessarily

00:27:04.810 --> 00:27:07.850
have it set up where there's
buttons because it's a surface.

00:27:07.850 --> 00:27:09.100
You could just use your voice.

00:27:09.100 --> 00:27:11.720
And so you don't need
a green button to go.

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:14.000
You just say go and just
kind of make that more

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:15.860
part of the visual elements.

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:17.360
And so an example
would be let's say

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:19.402
you have a game where
there's a little character.

00:27:19.402 --> 00:27:22.760
Instead of pushing buttons that
says attack, or go home, or X,

00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:25.190
Y, Z, just push on the
monster you want to attack.

00:27:25.190 --> 00:27:28.010
And push on the Home icon thing
in the background, so you could

00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:29.990
go and do it that way
instead, or, of course,

00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:32.060
use your voice to
control your character.

00:27:32.060 --> 00:27:35.305
So it's a different type
of UI experience as well.

00:27:35.305 --> 00:27:36.680
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So if you were

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:40.380
to pinpoint some of the use
cases for the listeners today,

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:42.650
what would be some
of the best use cases

00:27:42.650 --> 00:27:45.830
to kind of handle or
tackle as the beginners who

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:48.990
are starting to think about
creating something for voice?

00:27:48.990 --> 00:27:50.990
CATHY PEARL: Yeah, I mean,
as Jessica mentioned,

00:27:50.990 --> 00:27:53.180
one of the good
indicators is, do you

00:27:53.180 --> 00:27:55.850
have conversations with other
humans about this thing?

00:27:55.850 --> 00:27:57.743
Because that's often
a good indicator.

00:27:57.743 --> 00:27:58.910
Also of course, the context.

00:27:58.910 --> 00:28:00.050
Where is the person?

00:28:00.050 --> 00:28:02.650
Are they in the kitchen,
and their hands are busy?

00:28:02.650 --> 00:28:04.400
Or they're a new parent
and they're always

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:06.380
holding their baby, so they
don't have use of their hands.

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:07.970
Or they're in the
car and you really

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:09.920
need to reduce the
cognitive load.

00:28:09.920 --> 00:28:11.490
But you really have
to ask yourself,

00:28:11.490 --> 00:28:13.310
what is better
about someone doing

00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:17.180
this task through something like
a smart speaker than on an app?

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:18.073
Maybe it's not.

00:28:18.073 --> 00:28:20.240
And I think a lot of companies
get excited and like,

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:21.080
we're going to build for voice.

00:28:21.080 --> 00:28:22.020
It's going to be awesome.

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:23.600
And then it fails, and
they're like, I understand.

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:24.240
Why didn't it work?

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:25.948
And it's well, because
it's faster for me

00:28:25.948 --> 00:28:26.917
to do it on my phone.

00:28:26.917 --> 00:28:29.000
So you need to think through
some of these things,

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:31.910
like what's the value add,
truly, for doing something

00:28:31.910 --> 00:28:32.743
on a smart speaker?

00:28:32.743 --> 00:28:34.160
The other thing I
was going to say

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:37.730
is that a wonderful place to
be thinking about conversation

00:28:37.730 --> 00:28:40.490
design is really through the
idea of inclusive design, which

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:43.190
is we want to make sure
that whatever your product

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:45.830
or feature is or the
information you have available,

00:28:45.830 --> 00:28:47.360
can everyone access it?

00:28:47.360 --> 00:28:49.430
And voice technology is
really reaching the point

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:51.097
finally where it's
getting mature enough

00:28:51.097 --> 00:28:53.510
to help really the people
who need it the most.

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:55.850
Maybe somebody who is
visually impaired, somebody

00:28:55.850 --> 00:28:58.220
who has muscular
dystrophy and can't

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:00.960
use their hands to do things,
but they can use their voice.

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:02.480
And that's really
one of the places

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:05.370
I think right now voice
is the most powerful.

00:29:05.370 --> 00:29:07.370
But to me, this idea
of inclusive design

00:29:07.370 --> 00:29:10.002
isn't just about this one
group or that one group.

00:29:10.002 --> 00:29:11.960
It's really about all of
us, because all of us,

00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:14.427
throughout our lives, have
situational impairments,

00:29:14.427 --> 00:29:16.010
where maybe you're
walking in the door

00:29:16.010 --> 00:29:17.030
and you're holding
your groceries.

00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:18.650
And you briefly don't
have use of your hands.

00:29:18.650 --> 00:29:20.115
Or I can't find my
reading glasses,

00:29:20.115 --> 00:29:21.990
and I can't read this
important text message.

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:23.327
Or I broke my arm--

00:29:23.327 --> 00:29:24.160
all of these things.

00:29:24.160 --> 00:29:26.385
So really, it's about
building for everybody.

00:29:26.385 --> 00:29:28.760
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And do I
need to be a machine learning

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:31.010
expert, and do I need
to know natural language

00:29:31.010 --> 00:29:34.200
processing in order to
start building for voice?

00:29:34.200 --> 00:29:36.200
JESSICA DENE EARLEY-CHA:
No, which is fantastic.

00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:37.950
I know when I first
started in this field,

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:39.410
I was really
intimidated and scared

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:41.360
that I would have to
learn all these really

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:43.880
interesting concepts and
really challenging concepts.

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:47.820
And the time to get up to speed
on that would take a while.

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:50.600
And what's great is we provide
a tool called Dialogflow

00:29:50.600 --> 00:29:52.940
that does the NLU for you.

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:55.400
And so what's great is that
you just, as a developer,

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:58.220
identify the different
intents or functionalities

00:29:58.220 --> 00:30:00.710
that the user could potentially
have within your voice

00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:04.430
application and just provide
training phrases to specify

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:07.007
for each of those
possible interactions.

00:30:07.007 --> 00:30:08.840
And it handles the
machine learning for you.

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:11.015
So what's really great is
you don't need to do that,

00:30:11.015 --> 00:30:12.390
but if you're
interested in that,

00:30:12.390 --> 00:30:15.180
you can also connect Google
Assistant to your own NLU

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:15.680
as well.

00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:17.400
So you don't have
to use Dialogflow.

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:19.160
So if you do have that
background and that knowledge,

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:19.730
great.

00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:22.005
Connect it to any other
NLU that you have.

00:30:22.005 --> 00:30:23.630
But if you don't,
don't worry about it.

00:30:23.630 --> 00:30:24.770
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
it sounds like there's

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:26.478
a bunch of cool tools
out there if people

00:30:26.478 --> 00:30:28.920
want to get started, including
Dialogflow and, of course,

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:31.322
Priyanka, you've got the
Deconstructing Chat Bot series

00:30:31.322 --> 00:30:32.780
that goes into a
little bit of this

00:30:32.780 --> 00:30:35.230
and links out to a bunch
of other useful resources.

00:30:35.230 --> 00:30:36.230
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:30:36.230 --> 00:30:37.820
We also have a
bunch of code labs

00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:41.158
to refer to while you do the
Deconstructing Chat Bot videos.

00:30:41.158 --> 00:30:43.700
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So if people
are really interested in that,

00:30:43.700 --> 00:30:45.380
that sounds like a
good starting point.

00:30:45.380 --> 00:30:47.390
If people want to learn more
about the best practices,

00:30:47.390 --> 00:30:48.620
you mentioned the link earlier.

00:30:48.620 --> 00:30:50.495
Are there other good
resources for those best

00:30:50.495 --> 00:30:52.848
practices if we're thinking
about conversation design?

00:30:52.848 --> 00:30:54.890
CATHY PEARL: We've also
got some videos out there

00:30:54.890 --> 00:30:56.373
on things like
conversation repair.

00:30:56.373 --> 00:30:58.290
Also, Persona, we didn't
really touch on that.

00:30:58.290 --> 00:31:00.050
But when you are
designing, you want

00:31:00.050 --> 00:31:02.030
to be thinking about
the personality

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:04.100
and the characteristics
of your action

00:31:04.100 --> 00:31:05.807
or your conversational
experience.

00:31:05.807 --> 00:31:07.390
So Persona is another
important thing.

00:31:07.390 --> 00:31:09.832
So we have some videos
resources for that as well.

00:31:09.832 --> 00:31:11.540
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
what's in the future

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:12.950
for conversation design?

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:15.087
Because I saw this little
thing about Alter Ego.

00:31:15.087 --> 00:31:17.420
CATHY PEARL: There's some
pretty interesting prototyping

00:31:17.420 --> 00:31:18.530
work going on right now.

00:31:18.530 --> 00:31:22.100
Alter Ego is one example,
which is out of MIT Media Lab.

00:31:22.100 --> 00:31:23.960
And it's all around
the idea of a lot of us

00:31:23.960 --> 00:31:26.360
might be comfortable talking
to our smart speakers

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:29.660
in our own home, but maybe
not when we're on the bus,

00:31:29.660 --> 00:31:31.910
or out about on the street,
or even in our office,

00:31:31.910 --> 00:31:33.290
it could be a little
annoying to hear everybody

00:31:33.290 --> 00:31:34.640
talking to their computer.

00:31:34.640 --> 00:31:37.490
So there's this idea of silent
speech or sub vocalization,

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:40.950
which sounds very sci-fi, where
essentially, you are wearing

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:42.200
something along your jaw line.

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:45.350
And what it's doing is it's
picking up pre-speech signals.

00:31:45.350 --> 00:31:47.780
So as you start to form words,
before the words actually

00:31:47.780 --> 00:31:50.390
come out of your mouth, you're
doing these tiny little signals

00:31:50.390 --> 00:31:51.890
that are actually
indicating what it

00:31:51.890 --> 00:31:53.790
is that you're about to speak.

00:31:53.790 --> 00:31:55.310
And so this technology
is picking up

00:31:55.310 --> 00:31:58.337
on those pre-speech signals
and translating them silently

00:31:58.337 --> 00:32:00.170
into words, which could
then be passed along

00:32:00.170 --> 00:32:01.333
to a voice assistant.

00:32:01.333 --> 00:32:02.750
And of course, the
responses could

00:32:02.750 --> 00:32:05.850
be done in your headphones, so
no one would actually hear it.

00:32:05.850 --> 00:32:08.340
And I encourage people to
look at the Alter Ego video

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:10.320
that MIT Media Lab put
out to check it out.

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:12.000
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So that's
like an example of someone

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:14.040
taking this conversation
design and just

00:32:14.040 --> 00:32:17.083
shortcutting the actual
needing to talk part.

00:32:17.083 --> 00:32:18.750
CATHY PEARL: Well,
you're still talking.

00:32:18.750 --> 00:32:21.018
It's just that the
words are not audible.

00:32:21.018 --> 00:32:22.560
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Which to me is what

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:24.385
I would define talking as, but.

00:32:24.385 --> 00:32:26.760
CATHY PEARL: Speaking, maybe
I should say, or conversing.

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:28.260
I don't know what
the right verb is,

00:32:28.260 --> 00:32:29.885
but you're still
having a conversation.

00:32:29.885 --> 00:32:31.468
But you can think
of it just like when

00:32:31.468 --> 00:32:33.720
you're typing to somebody,
you're talking to somebody.

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:36.320
But the words are not
spoken out loud, so.

00:32:36.320 --> 00:32:38.820
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I mean, it
could open up a lot of avenues,

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:41.250
it sounds like, for-- almost
like an additional interface

00:32:41.250 --> 00:32:42.625
to communicate
with these things.

00:32:42.625 --> 00:32:44.790
But I'm guessing it
would still follow

00:32:44.790 --> 00:32:47.830
a lot of the same conversation
best practices and guides.

00:32:47.830 --> 00:32:49.538
CATHY PEARL: For sure,
and even if you're

00:32:49.538 --> 00:32:51.810
talking to, say, a chat
bot that's typing only,

00:32:51.810 --> 00:32:53.910
you're still following
the same best practices.

00:32:53.910 --> 00:32:55.710
And when you type
to a chat bot, it's

00:32:55.710 --> 00:32:57.810
much more like
spoken words than it

00:32:57.810 --> 00:33:00.640
is if you're writing a formal
essay or something like that.

00:33:00.640 --> 00:33:02.790
So even though it's
a different modality,

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:05.395
you're still following
conversational principles.

00:33:05.395 --> 00:33:07.770
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And what
