00:35:28.140 --> 00:35:30.996
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:34.403
For this, you
published a blog post.

00:35:34.403 --> 00:35:35.820
I wanted to ask
you more about it.

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:39.240
You have a solution
here to combine

00:35:39.240 --> 00:35:41.220
Dialogflow and BigQuery.

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:43.390
And I mean, my first
question, of course,

00:35:43.390 --> 00:35:45.190
is, why would you do that?

00:35:45.190 --> 00:35:47.580
What tools are available in
there that make that useful,

00:35:47.580 --> 00:35:49.175
but then also, how do you do it?

00:35:49.175 --> 00:35:50.550
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So when I have

00:35:50.550 --> 00:35:53.640
been speaking with customers
and users of Dialogflow,

00:35:53.640 --> 00:35:55.710
one of the cool questions
that always comes up

00:35:55.710 --> 00:35:57.630
is, I want to take
some of the data

00:35:57.630 --> 00:36:00.060
that I'm getting from the users.

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:03.000
Like, I take an example of
an appointment schedule,

00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:04.830
and the time and
the date and why

00:36:04.830 --> 00:36:06.990
people want to set an
appointment for coming

00:36:06.990 --> 00:36:11.070
to whatever it is that I
serve as a service, right?

00:36:11.070 --> 00:36:13.710
And I just wrote
this cool little tool

00:36:13.710 --> 00:36:16.470
to understand there is the
reporting side of things

00:36:16.470 --> 00:36:20.190
that Dialogflow offers, but
there's always an analysis

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:23.310
that you may want to do as a
business in terms of what you

00:36:23.310 --> 00:36:25.905
want to make decisions off of.

00:36:25.905 --> 00:36:29.670
So I take these appointment
things from the users--

00:36:29.670 --> 00:36:31.740
date, time, and why
they want to come in.

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:33.840
And then I want to
do my own analysis

00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:36.660
to understand when do
people come in more,

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:38.520
or what are the different
requests that I'm

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:40.020
getting more versus less.

00:36:40.020 --> 00:36:44.130
And all of that is very
easily available as variables,

00:36:44.130 --> 00:36:45.720
and I can pass
them into BigQuery

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:47.530
and I can run SQL
commands on it.

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:51.720
So it's a very good way to find
out how can I provide a better

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:53.220
customer experience.

00:36:53.220 --> 00:36:55.890
And what are some
of the questions

00:36:55.890 --> 00:36:59.280
I should even automate or
not automate is an indication

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:02.080
that you can get with an
integration like this.

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:04.470
So that's the why
aspect of why I

00:37:04.470 --> 00:37:07.260
came to think that this
could be a good tool.

00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:08.403
And how do you do it?

00:37:08.403 --> 00:37:09.570
It's actually pretty simple.

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:12.390
In Dialogflow, you
have the fulfillment

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:14.400
where you can write
Node.js code to make

00:37:14.400 --> 00:37:16.750
a connection to any API.

00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:20.910
So in this case, I'm using
the BigQuery API to take those

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:23.940
variables that I'm capturing
as a part of Dialogflow--

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:26.250
the dates, and the times,
and why people want to come

00:37:26.250 --> 00:37:27.630
in for this appointment--

00:37:27.630 --> 00:37:30.810
and then just pass those
variables into a table

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:33.840
by calling my BigQuery
API from the Cloud

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:37.350
Function that is inside the
fulfillment from Dialogflow.

00:37:37.350 --> 00:37:39.420
So it's pretty
simple integration.

00:37:39.420 --> 00:37:42.690
If you want to look at a sample
or want to do it yourself,

00:37:42.690 --> 00:37:46.290
I have linked some of
the code into GitHub.

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:48.983
And you can download that
from the repository as well.

00:37:48.983 --> 00:37:50.400
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So you still get

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:52.682
to use the dialog
aspect of Dialogflow

00:37:52.682 --> 00:37:54.390
in creating the chat
bot, but then you're

00:37:54.390 --> 00:37:57.510
easily able to take a
question that somebody asks

00:37:57.510 --> 00:37:59.400
or some information
that somebody provides,

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:02.100
and then basically query against
BigQuery, which is really

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:05.940
good at, well, big queries,
and then take that, get

00:38:05.940 --> 00:38:09.150
an answer back, and then use
that back in your Dialogflow

00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:09.650
application.

00:38:09.650 --> 00:38:11.108
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
You could either

00:38:11.108 --> 00:38:13.410
use that back in your
Dialogflow application,

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:15.570
or you could take
that information

00:38:15.570 --> 00:38:20.500
and create your business
strategy off of it and be like,

00:38:20.500 --> 00:38:26.040
OK, now I should change my bot,
or I should add these questions

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:29.860
because people are asking for
these more, that type of stuff.

00:38:29.860 --> 00:38:33.930
So you can decide where to
focus more of your energy on.

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:36.630
But as you said, you
can also process it

00:38:36.630 --> 00:38:39.000
and send the response
back to the Dialogflow

00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:41.330
to have further
conversation, so both ways.

00:38:41.330 --> 00:38:43.622
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah, would
